{"vid":"KCCA22ngoPU","source":"KCCA22ngoPU.en-en.json3","lang":"en","cues":[{"t":0,"txt":"RON FRIEDMAN: Over the last few years, my team and I have done a deep dive on what high performing"},{"t":4.2,"txt":"teams do differently. We surveyed thousands of workers, and asked them two key questions about"},{"t":8.2,"txt":"their teams. Number one, how effective is your team at meeting its goals? And number two is,"},{"t":12.6,"txt":"compared to other teams in your industry, how would you rate your team's performance?"},{"t":16.9,"txt":"Then we took the teams with a perfect score, a very tiny group we call super teams,"},{"t":20.7,"txt":"and looked at what are the super teams doing differently on a day-to-day basis? And we"},{"t":24.6,"txt":"looked at everything from how they structure their day, to how they run their meetings,"},{"t":28.6,"txt":"to even how they recover in the evenings and weekends. The patterns were striking."},{"t":37.1,"txt":"[MUSIC PLAYING]"},{"t":37.1,"txt":"Superteams share three key strengths. The first is they get more done by better managing their time,"},{"t":44.1,"txt":"energy, and attention. The second is they don't just collaborate well,"},{"t":48.4,"txt":"they actively make one another better. And the third is that they're not satisfied doing"},{"t":53.8,"txt":"great work. They're constantly building their skills and improving over time."},{"t":58.2,"txt":"In today's discussion, what we're going to cover is the latter of the"},{"t":60.4,"txt":"three strengths how super teams get better over time. At most companies,"},{"t":65.1,"txt":"getting better is surprisingly difficult, and that's because improvement requires"},{"t":68.6,"txt":"two things that most businesses are not particularly comfortable with--"},{"t":71.8,"txt":"and that is risk taking and failure. Trying something new often involves making mistakes."},{"t":77.9,"txt":"Unless the team feels comfortable making those mistakes, learning simply becomes impossible."},{"t":82.7,"txt":"There are a few tactics that I often see on superteams. The first is that they are"},{"t":88.2,"txt":"constantly experimenting. The other thing that they are very high on, is they're very high"},{"t":92.1,"txt":"on something called feedback seeking. And the third thing is that they reward innovation. And"},{"t":96.6,"txt":"so we tend to see more people trying new things, because there's more of an opportunity for people"},{"t":101.5,"txt":"who come up with new ideas to incorporate what they find into their career path."},{"t":107.9,"txt":"An experiment is a low, small risk test that allows your team to learn something"},{"t":113.7,"txt":"new before committing to a decision. By definition, if you know it's going to work,"},{"t":119,"txt":"it's not an experiment. You're exchanging efficiency for information. So even if it"},{"t":123.7,"txt":"doesn't work out, even if the experiment is a failure, you're going to learn something"},{"t":127.6,"txt":"that helps you improve in the future. I think that there are simple things that"},{"t":131.8,"txt":"any leader can do to incorporate this learning and experimentation mindset,"},{"t":135.9,"txt":"and part of it is having a weekly review of experiments that are ongoing, regardless of"},{"t":140.2,"txt":"whether they were successful or failures. The idea is to socialize some of these findings"},{"t":147,"txt":"so that people appreciate that this is something that is a routine way in which this team operates."},{"t":152.1,"txt":"One of the things we from the research is that 85% turns out to be a pretty good"},{"t":157.1,"txt":"indicator. You're looking for 85% success. It tells you you're generally successful,"},{"t":161.8,"txt":"but it also tells you you're failing a small portion of the time-- and that is"},{"t":165.2,"txt":"suggestive of someone who is stretching, while still performing at a high level."},{"t":173,"txt":"One of the fascinating findings in our research on superteams is that the quantity of feedback that"},{"t":178.9,"txt":"they receive on a regular basis is much higher than those on average teams. And part of it is"},{"t":183,"txt":"because the leader is delivering feedback more frequently, and they're doing it in a way that"},{"t":187.3,"txt":"is motivating rather than judgmental. But on superteams, people get more feedback from their"},{"t":194,"txt":"colleagues. And it's not because their colleagues are pointing out mistakes that they're making."},{"t":198,"txt":"It's because they're sharing their work early, they're getting input from their colleagues,"},{"t":202.3,"txt":"and they're making revisions before the work gets to the boss or to the client."},{"t":207.5,"txt":"There are a couple of things that leaders can do to get their team higher in feedback"},{"t":210.7,"txt":"seeking. The first thing is, focus on the future, not the past. The second thing is,"},{"t":216.4,"txt":"don't provide a laundry list of all the things they need to change."},{"t":219.5,"txt":"Focus on one small thing they can change to get better in the future."},{"t":223.1,"txt":"And the third thing is, when possible, try to make it a conversation. Don't just say,"},{"t":227.3,"txt":"here's what I need you to do. Say, here's what I think might help in the future. What do you"},{"t":232.5,"txt":"think? That's the kind of feedback that gets people feeling energized rather than judged."},{"t":239.6,"txt":"One of the things that superteams do really well is they reward innovation."},{"t":243.2,"txt":"And my favorite example of this is GM, the company that is best known for Post-Its and"},{"t":248.2,"txt":"for Scotch Tape. They've been around for over 120 years. The question is, how do you create"},{"t":253.7,"txt":"a company that is so effective for so long? One of their secrets is rewarding innovation."},{"t":260.6,"txt":"At 3M, they have a policy called the 30% rule. In order for a division to earn its bonus, at"},{"t":267.8,"txt":"least 30% of its revenue must come from a product introduced in the last four years. And I love this"},{"t":274.5,"txt":"approach, because it ensures that even if things are going well, your team will not coast, because"},{"t":278.5,"txt":"they're constantly worried about what's next. One of the keys is rewarding intelligent failure. And"},{"t":284.5,"txt":"what that refers is the idea that when someone takes a real risk and it doesn't work out,"},{"t":289.4,"txt":"they get the same level of reward as someone whose experiment resulted in a giant success."},{"t":293.7,"txt":"I think unless you're seeing some degree of failure or setbacks, what it suggests is not"},{"t":300.2,"txt":"that everything is going perfectly, but you're not stretching your skills or developing in an"},{"t":305.9,"txt":"ongoing way. Part of what leaders need to let go of is the desire for perfection,"},{"t":310.1,"txt":"and instead embrace the progress mentality-- and that requires portfolio thinking. You're"},{"t":315.9,"txt":"going to have some degree of risk, and that's what's going to lead to"},{"t":319.4,"txt":"ongoing improvement. But if you have no risk and everything is going perfectly,"},{"t":323.6,"txt":"it doesn't mean you're getting everything right. It means you're falling behind."},{"t":328.6,"txt":"[MUSIC PLAYING]"}],"words":[{"t":0,"w":"RON"},{"t":0,"w":"FRIEDMAN:"},{"t":0,"w":"Over"},{"t":0,"w":"the"},{"t":0,"w":"last"},{"t":0,"w":"few"},{"t":0,"w":"years,"},{"t":0,"w":"my"},{"t":0,"w":"team"},{"t":0,"w":"and"},{"t":0,"w":"I"},{"t":0,"w":"have"},{"t":0,"w":"done"},{"t":0,"w":"a"},{"t":0,"w":"deep"},{"t":0,"w":"dive"},{"t":0,"w":"on"},{"t":0,"w":"what"},{"t":0,"w":"high"},{"t":0,"w":"performing"},{"t":4.24,"w":"teams"},{"t":4.24,"w":"do"},{"t":4.24,"w":"differently."},{"t":4.24,"w":"We"},{"t":4.24,"w":"surveyed"},{"t":4.24,"w":"thousands"},{"t":4.24,"w":"of"},{"t":4.24,"w":"workers,"},{"t":4.24,"w":"and"},{"t":4.24,"w":"asked"},{"t":4.24,"w":"them"},{"t":4.24,"w":"two"},{"t":4.24,"w":"key"},{"t":4.24,"w":"questions"},{"t":4.24,"w":"about"},{"